  "body": "In #337 / e86b2e4b8fdef45404c61c1a85c40da7edf8b561 @annevk removed all origin checks from the `Worker` and `SharedWorker` constructors, with the rational that fetch checking if the url of the script being fetched is same origin is good enough. This doesn't work for shared workers though:\n\nFirst of all the result of this is that no same-origin checks of any kind are done if a `SharedWorkerGlobalScope` object already exists with matching name and script url. According to the spec http://evil.com/ creating a `SharedWorker('https://google.com/worker.js')` is supposed to succeed if such a worker already happens to be running.\n\nSimilarly with module scripts it is now okay in some situations to have a scriptURL that is not same-origin. In these cases matching with existing `SharedWorkerGlobalScope` instances presumably is still supposed to take the origin into account, even though the current spec text doesn't.\n\n(also fwiw it doesn't seem like either firefox or chrome ever fully implemented the changes in e86b2e4b8fdef45404c61c1a85c40da7edf8b561. Chrome still seems to throw a SecurityError DOMException for non-same-origin scripts, while firefox still seems to do so for Worker, just not for SharedWorker).\n\ncc/ @domenic \n",
